<section class="section" id="conclusions">
  <h2>6. Conclusions & Limitations</h2>

  <h3>Key Findings</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The exam structure has not remained entirely stable.</strong> While MCQs (15) and Long Questions (3) have been fixed constants, Short Questions underwent two significant changes — doubling from 9 to 18 in 2017, then settling at 15 from 2021 onward. The current 15/15/3 format has been stable for five years (2021–2025).</li>
    <li><strong>The 2017 expansion was the single largest structural shift.</strong> The doubling of Short Questions increased the total paper from 27 to 36 questions, shifting the balance from MCQ-dominated to Short-Question-dominated.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-pandemic stabilization is evident.</strong> From 2021 onward, the exam has maintained a consistent structure (33 total questions), suggesting the board has settled on a format it considers balanced.</li>
    <li><strong>C Programming and Database/DBMS are the two pillars</strong> of the examination, with C programming receiving greater emphasis. Within C, loops, functions, operators, and control structures are the most tested concepts.</li>
    <li><strong>The topic scope is gradually expanding.</strong> Recent years (2023–2025) introduce advanced concepts like pointers, bitwise operators, ACID properties, ER diagrams, and binary search — suggesting the syllabus is evolving to include more sophisticated material.</li>
    <li><strong>Certain question patterns are highly predictable.</strong> Full-form acronym questions, output-tracing questions, and "write a program to…" questions appear with near-certainty every year.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>2020 data is missing.</strong> The dataset contains no questions for 2020, likely due to COVID-19 exam cancellations or deferrals. Any trend analysis crossing this gap should be interpreted with caution.</li>
    <li><strong>No distinction between annual and supplementary papers.</strong> Some years may include questions from both examination sessions, but this distinction is not encoded in the data. This could inflate counts for certain years if both sessions are represented.</li>
    <li><strong>Community-compiled dataset.</strong> This is not an official BIEK publication. Minor transcription errors, omitted questions, or misattributed years may exist.</li>
    <li><strong>Small dataset size (327 rows).</strong> While sufficient for broad pattern detection, the limited sample size reduces statistical power for fine-grained year-by-year topic-level trend analysis.</li>
    <li><strong>Dollar-sign delimiter in question text.</strong> The <code>$</code> character separates MCQ options and long-question sub-parts. Topic analysis accounts for this, but some multi-part questions may be undercounted if sub-parts cover distinct topics.</li>
    <li><strong>No marks allocation data.</strong> The dataset records question counts but not marks per question. Since Long Questions carry significantly more marks than MCQs, the true assessment weight differs from the question-count proportions shown in this report.</li>
    <li><strong>Paper-II only.</strong> This analysis covers only Paper-II (theory). Paper-I and practical examination content are not included.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="insight-box blue">
    <strong>Final Assessment:</strong> The BIEK Computer Science 12th Class examination has evolved from a compact 27-question format (2015–2016) through an expanded 36-question phase (2017–2019) to a stabilized 33-question format (2021–2025). While the overall structure has settled, the <em>content</em> of the examination continues to evolve — with recent years introducing more advanced programming and database concepts. Educators should plan for this ongoing enrichment while anchoring preparation in the consistently high-frequency topics identified in this report.
  </div>
</section>

